Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 12:57:09 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: Nutrition was: topics?

> From: cbmvax!snark.thyrsus.com!esr@uunet.uu.net (Eric S. Raymond)
> 
> > I'm not a vegetarian . . . yet.
> 
> I respect anyone's choice to be a vegetarian.  However, I also feel a need
> to point out, quietly, that this path is not for everyone.  The energy
> density of vegetables is much lower than animal products.  If you live in a
> very cold climate, vegetarianism can almost literally kill you.  Even in
> our climate, I know people who tried going vegan and had to stop when they
> realized they were slowly starving to death.

I'm sorry, Eric, but this is silly and not borne out by the evidence.
If you need more calories, you can just eat more. Most people's bodies are
structured to give them very clear signals that they aren't eating enough.
I have no trouble getting enough calories. I'll admit that I tend to eat
a lot more bulk than non-vegetarians, but this has never been a problem.

> Also, there are critical trace elements and some B vitamins that you have to get
> either from animal products or supplements.  A friend of my brother's neglected
> the supplements and screwed up his immune system so badly that a minor grease
> burn on his arm went necrotic and turned into a major lymphatic-system
> infection.  He nearly lost the arm.

I also have a great deal of difficulty believing that the doctors in this
case weren't crazy. There is only one nutrient that isn't present in
a pure vegan diet -- B12. We used to get B12 from traces of soil contaminants
and apparently have become too clean for our own good. However, B12 is
required in extraordinarily small amounts -- about five micrograms per day.
It is also stored for enormously long periods. Someone who was eating meat
regularly likely has a five to ten year supply stored up in his body.
Lastly, the symptoms of B12 deficiency start with bad neural damage, not
immune system problems. Your friend of your brother would have had to
have been a twitching hulk before his immune system would be compromised
sufficiently from the lack of B12 for the events you describe to have occured.
This is not to say that immune systems can't become compromised -- its just
that the thesis that it was from a lack of B12 sounds fishy.

Myself, I take no chances -- every few months I use a nasal membrane applicator
with a dose of about 500 mcg of B12 -- which given the superior absorbtion
versus the digestive tract should be enough for me for half a year. I also
take a daily vitamin supplement that contains about 6mcg of B12, so between
the two of these I should be very safe.

An article in Vegetarian Times that I saw recently, however, contends that
B12 deficiency is extraordinarily rare among vegans. They also recommend
taking a supplement just in case, but they don't seem to think its a big
issue, and their nutrition reporting is usually impecable.

> Human beings are not really designed to be vegetarians --- our dentition,
> digestion, and dietary requirements are those of omnivores.

Quite false, actually. True omnivores like Bears have much shorter digestive
tracts and teeth designed for eating meat. Humans CAN digest meat, but
we don't seem to be built for a true omnivorous diet. Our gut length, teeth
and other factors seem to mark us out as creatures primarily designed
for a vegetarian diet with the capacity to eat meat when it is available.

> Vegetarianism
> is within our adaptive envelope, but just barely; unsupplemented lacto-ovo is
> OK if you're careful, but veganism requires technological help.

This seems to be completely false. As I've said, there only seems to be one
vitamin a vegan diet lacks, and our ancestors likely got it from non-animal
sources anyway. There are cultures around the world that live on nearly
vegan diets and are perfectly healthy.

I'd say its the meat eaters that have to be careful -- and I'd also say
that the technological help required to manage having your coronary arteries
completely occluded by athrosclerotic plaques is far greater than that
required to survive on my diet.

> (Me?  Happy omnivore --- I live, effectively, on raw vegetables and (cooked)
> meat.  My own version of dietary virtue is avoiding heavily processed foods.
> I've tried cutting my meat intake as an experiment; it made me hungry all the
> time.

Why didn't you just eat more?

Perry

