Date: Tue, 26 Jan 93 13:09:19 EST
From: pmetzger@shearson.com (Perry E. Metzger)
Subject: Nutrition was: topics?

> From: Blair Haworth <wbh@mail.lib.duke.edu>
> 
>           Yes indeed.  I will confess to a negative gut reaction (as
>           it were; sorry...) to a lot of this diet talk, which seems
>           (this is just sybaritic old me, mind you) to chase the flat
>           part of the diminishing returns curve.

I'd disagree. Once you've quit smoking, your biggest barrier to living
to a ripe old age is dietary generated disease -- heart disease, stroke,
colon cancer, etc, etc. I'd say the returns are very high.

>           To paraphrase Emma Goldman:  If I can't eat cheeseburgers,
>           I don't want to be part of your Singularity.

Thats fine. Just be aware that you are making a conscious decision
to trade probable life span for cheeseburgers. Myself, I never really
liked cheeseburgers that much, so I don't feel that it was much
of a decision to make.

>           High-minded types will doubtless warn me against swapping my
>           birthright for a mess of cheeseburgers at this point.  Ah
>           well.

Hey, its perfectly fine to make such a tradeoff -- just be well aware that
this is not theoretical and that you may regret the decision when you
feel a crushing pain in your chest twenty years down the line. If you
truly are willing to risk dying over your diet, then by all means, pick the
tradeoff that makes you happiest.

Perry


