Date: Tue, 26 Jan 1993 14:04:00 -0500
From: Elizabeth Schwartz <betsys@ra.cs.umb.edu>
Subject: DIET: too much protein?

If Dasher's sister is replacing simple sugars in her diet with
protein, she may be cutting down on her blood sugar swings, at the
expense of her arteries and kidneys. I wonder what would happen if she
focused on a diet consisting of mostly whole grains, beans, and
vegetables? 
   "Diet for a New America" (my poorly written book with the great
footnotes here) cites three studies showing that increased consumption
of fat screws up blood sugar maintenance levels and that diets low in
both sugar and fat can help prevent or reduce hypoglycemia. I'll post
if anyone wants the citations. There does seem to be an awful lot of
evidence suggesting that both hypoglycemia and adult-onset diabetes
can be prevented by diet.
   Betsy

PS re cheeseburgers: I lived in a vegetarian co-op for three years;
two of those years we had a very good cook who was on a very low-salt
low-fat diet. I used to go out about once a month and spring for a
double-cheeseburger platter and fries with extra salt, which was
enough to "balance" my diet. 
   I've been eating a vegetarian diet with cheese recently.


